# The age RECIPIENT for the nightly backup — the public half, and only the public half.
#
# THE REAL RECIPIENT IS NOW COMMITTED, at `deploy/backup/age.recipient` — Klas generated the
# identity on his own machine 2026-08-09, and only this public half has ever left it. This file
# remains as the format note and as the standing warning below.
#
# Install the tracked recipient to /opt/jobbliggaren/deploy/backup/age.recipient, root-owned and
# 0444. jobbliggaren-backup.sh refuses to run without it, and refuses a value that does not look
# like an age recipient.
#
# ONE LINE, STARTING age1. It is the output of `age-keygen`'s "Public key:" line.
#
#   age1<52 more bech32 characters>       <- SHAPE ONLY. A well-formed sample value used to sit
#                                            here, and the script accepts any well-formed one:
#                                            installing the sample by mistake would encrypt every
#                                            night to a key nobody holds, silently, until the
#                                            drill. The real value is in `age.recipient` beside
#                                            this file and the runbook verifies against it.
#
# THE PRIVATE KEY (AGE-SECRET-KEY-…) MUST NEVER EXIST ON THIS BOX, in this directory or any
# other. ADR 0050 `Amendment 2026-08-04` §7 requirement (b): a private key stored with the
# ciphertext makes the encryption decoration against theft of the same device. Encryption needs
# only the recipient, so the box has no reason to hold the other half — which is why requirement
# (b) is satisfied structurally here rather than by a rule anyone has to remember.
# jobbliggaren-backup.sh rejects an AGE-SECRET-KEY- value by name, as a cheap guard against a
# paste into the wrong file.
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT A SECRET, BUT ITS INTEGRITY MATTERS. An attacker who swaps the recipient for
# one they hold gets every subsequent night's data and costs us restorability of those nights —
# silently, because nothing on the box can decrypt what it produced. Only the restore drill
# detects it. That is one of the reasons the drill is periodic rather than one-off; see
# docs/runbooks/backup-restore.md §6.
#
# Where the private half lives, and its escrow, are Klas's to decide and are NOT settled — the
# same open gate as the master key's escrow (vps-deploy-stack.md §5 row 26). A backup whose key
# is not escrowed is not a backup.
